## Why This Is Asked

Tests for multi-tenant isolation are foundational for SaaS apps. The goal is to confirm UI and API boundaries prevent cross-tenant data leakage, a common subtle bug.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-tenancy data isolation
- Role-based access control (RBAC)
- Deterministic fixtures for repeatable runs
- UI vs API boundary testing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Cypress skeleton for tenant isolation
describe('Tenant isolation', () => {
  beforeEach(() => {
    seedTenants(['A','B']);
  });
  it('creates user in A and enforces isolation', () => {
    cy.visit('/login');
    cy.login('adminA','A');
    cy.visit('/admin/users');
    cy.get('[data-cy=create-user]').click();
    cy.get('[data-cy=username]').type('newuser');
    cy.get('[data-cy=save]').click();
    cy.contains('newuser').should('exist');
    cy.request({method:'GET', url:'/api/users?tenant=A'}).then(resp => {
      expect(resp.body.some(u => u.username==='newuser')).to.be.true;
    });
    cy.request({method:'GET', url:'/api/users?tenant=B'}).then(resp => {
      expect(resp.body.some(u => u.username==='newuser')).to.be.false;
    });
  });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale this for 100 tenants while keeping tests fast?
- How would you handle flaky network simulations and ensure determinism across runs?

## Why This Is Asked
Streaming UI tests are a common beginner-to-intermediate challenge in E2E testing. This question assesses the ability to design a reproducible test flow that exercises real-time updates, cancel/retry semantics, and test isolation—areas that frequently cause test flakiness in practice.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end streaming validation (SSE/WebSocket)
- Progressive rendering and ordering of chunks
- Cancel and retry behavior testing
- Test data isolation with sandboxed backends
- Network conditions simulation (throttling, outages)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright test sketch (pseudo-implementation)
test('streaming playground flow', async ({ page }) => {
  // Setup mock backend with isolated test data
  await setupTestBackend({ isolated: true });

  // User interactions
  await page.fill('[data-testid="prompt-input"]', 'Hello world');
  await page.selectOption('[data-testid="model-select"]', 'gpt-4');
  await page.fill('[data-testid="temperature"]', '0.7');

  // Start streaming and verify chunks
  const runButton = page.locator('[data-testid="run-button"]');
  await runButton.click();

  // Verify progressive rendering
  await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="output-pane"]'))
    .toContainText('Hello', { timeout: 1000 });
  await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="output-pane"]'))
    .toContainText('Hello world', { timeout: 2000 });

  // Test cancel behavior
  await page.locator('[data-testid="cancel-button"]').click();
  await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="output-pane"]'))
    .not.toContainText('complete');

  // Test retry with different parameters
  await page.selectOption('[data-testid="model-select"]', 'gpt-3.5');
  await runButton.click();
  await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="output-pane"]'))
    .toContainText('Hello world', { timeout: 3000 });
});
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests for offline-first flows are common in modern web apps and require practical use of network emulation, event queues, and deterministic conflict handling. This question probes hands-on ability to design reliable E2E tests that stay robust under connectivity changes.

## Key Concepts

- Offline-first UX and service workers
- Network emulation in Playwright or Cypress
- Backend API mocking and idempotent sync
- Deterministic conflict resolution policy
- Data seeding for repeatable tests

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright test skeleton
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('offline create and sync with deterministic merge', async ({ browser }) => {
  const context = await browser.newContext();
  const page = await context.newPage();
  // seed data via API (omitted for brevity)
  // go offline
  await context.setOffline(true);
  await page.goto('https://app.example');
  await page.fill('#title', 'Offline note');
  await page.fill('#body', 'content');
  await page.click('#save');
  await expect(page.locator('#status')).toHaveText('Queued');
  // go online and sync
  await context.setOffline(false);
  const resp = await page.waitForResponse('/api/notes');
  // assume server returns created note with serverId
  await expect(resp.status()).toBe(201);
  // verify note appears and is synced
  await expect(page.locator('.note-title').last()).toHaveText('Offline note');
  // optional conflict scenario in a second tab would be tested similarly
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this test for multiple concurrent offline edits across tabs?
- How would you ensure test reliability when the backend response latency varies?

